<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>How long do you think it would take Priya to make a trip of 90 miles if the linear relationship continues? 
    </item>
</list>
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
 
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Students may misunderstand how to interpret negative correlation coefficients. Ask students how the sign of the correlation coefficient is related to the linear model for the situation. Students may benefit from drawing an example scatter plot representing the situation.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>The purpose of this discussion is for students to understand that the correlation coefficient quantifies the strength of a linear relationship.</para>
<para>Introduce the terms positive and negative relationship. Give some guidelines as to when to call a relationship strong. For example: When \(| r | ≥ 0.8\), there is a strong, linear relationship; when \(| r | ≤ 0.5\), the relationship is weak; and when \(0.5 &lt; | r | &lt; 0.8\), the relationship is moderately strong. Although these are good guidelines, they should not be treated as a rule. Context is also important when determining whether to call a relationship strong or weak.</para>
<para>In general, \(r\) is related to how much of an improvement a linear model is over just using the mean as an estimate for the data.</para>
<para>Here are some questions for discussion:</para>
<list>
  <item>“How would you describe the relationship between distance and time for Priya’s trips? Be prepared to show your reasoning.” (It is a strong positive relationship. It is strong because \(r = 0.99\), and it is positive because time, \(y\), tends to increase as distance, \(x\), increases.)</item>
  <item>Refer students to scatter plot D from the warm up. “How would you describe the relationship between wait time and customer satisfaction? The correlation coefficient is \(r = - 0.52\).” (It is a negative relationship. It is a moderately strong relationship since the linear model is an improvement over merely using the mean for the data, but it is not a great improvement.)</item>
</list>
<para>       <figure><media alt="Scatter plot.   "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ade3ef85b629664d8fd3360aca8be534568d6e2f"/></media></figure></para>
<list>
  <item>“What is an example of data with a strong, negative relationship?” (An example of a strong negative relationship could be the relationship between the time it takes you to run a mile and your speed. As your speed increases, the time decreases.)</item>
</list>

<!--BEGIN ELL AND SWD GRAY BOX -->
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Conversing, Representing<title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Conversing, Representing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
      <para>Use this routine to amplify mathematical uses of language to describe the strength of linear models. After students share a response, invite them to repeat their reasoning using mathematical language relevant to the lesson, such as strong, weak, positive, or negative. For example, if students forget to use the desired language, ask, “Would you say that the relationship is strong or weak?” or “Would you say it is positive or negative?” For students who benefit from greater language support, ask, “Can you say that again, using the phrase ‘strong, positive relationship’?” Consider inviting the remaining students to repeat these phrases to provide additional opportunities for all students to produce this language.</para>
      <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Support sense-making</para>
</div>
</div>
<!--END ELL AND SWD GRAY BOX --> 

<title>3.5.2: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Determine the correlation coefficient of the data in the table below to 3 decimal places and interpret the value.</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry>8</entry>
      <entry>15</entry>
      <entry>26</entry>
      <entry>31</entry>
      <entry>56</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
      <entry>23</entry>
      <entry>41</entry>
      <entry>53</entry>
      <entry>72</entry>
      <entry>103</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(r = -0.987\); there is a weak negative relationship.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope of the points in the table is positive, so the correlation coefficient should be positive. \(r\)-values close to 1 or –1 show strong relationships. The answer is \(r=0.987\); there is a strong positive relationship.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(r=0.987\); there is a weak positive relationship.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: \(r\)-values close to 1 indicate a strong relationship. The answer is \(r=0.987\); there is a strong positive relationship.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(r=0.987\); there is a strong positive relationship.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Using graphing technology, ensure that you enter the data correctly. The slope of the points in the table is positive and the points are close to the line, so the correlation coefficient should be a positive number close to 1.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(r=-0.987\); there is a strong negative relationship.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope of the points in the table is positive, so the correlation coefficient should be positive. The answer is \(r=0.987\).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>3.5.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Calculating and Interpreting Correlation Coefficients</title>
<para>Properties of the correlation coefficient: </para>
<list>
  <item> Property 1: The sign of \(r\) (positive or negative) corresponds to the direction of the linear relationship. </item>
  <item> Property 2: A value of \(r = +1\) indicates a perfect positive linear relationship, with all points in the scatter plot falling exactly on a straight line. </item>
  <item> Property 3: A value of \(r = -1\) indicates a perfect negative linear relationship, with all points in the scatter plot falling exactly on a straight line. </item>
  <item> Property 4: The closer the value of \(r\) is to \(+1\) or \(-1\), the stronger the linear relationship.</item>
</list>
<para>Using the data of shoe length in inches and height in inches for 10 men, find the correlation coefficient of the data with graphing technology.</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\) (Shoe Length)</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(y\) (Height)</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>12.6</entry>
      <entry>74</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>11.8</entry>
      <entry>65</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>12.2</entry>
      <entry>71</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>11.6</entry>
      <entry>67</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>12.2</entry>
      <entry>69</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>11.4</entry>
      <entry>68</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>12.8</entry>
      <entry>70</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>12.2</entry>
      <entry>69</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>12.6</entry>
      <entry>72</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>11.8</entry>
      <entry>71</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para/>
<para>Based on the table, there is a moderate positive linear relationship between shoe length and height.</para>
<para>The table below shows how you can informally interpret the value of a correlation coefficient.</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">If the value of the correlation coefficient is . . .</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">You can say that . . .</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(r=1.0\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a perfect positive linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(0.7\leq r&lt;1.0\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a strong positive linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(0.3\leq r&lt;0.7\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a moderate positive linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(0&lt;r&lt;0.3\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a weak positive linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(r=0\)</entry>
      <entry>There is no linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(-0.3&lt;r&lt;0\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a weak negative linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(-0.7&lt;r\leq-0.3\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a moderate negative linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(-1.0&lt;r\leq-0.7\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a strong negative linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(r=-1.0\)</entry>
      <entry>There is a perfect negative linear relationship.</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>Try It: Calculating and Interpreting Correlation Coefficients</title>
